          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p2\",\"quote\":\"A Kepler orbit is an idealized, mathematical approximation of the orbit at a particular time.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p4\",\"quote\":\"Orbital elements can be obtained from orbital state vectors (position and velocity vectors of the orbiting object) by manual transformations or with computer software through a process known as orbit determination .\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p7\",\"quote\":\"Such a set of 6 elements, however, only describes the starting position of the orbiting object and the shape of its trajectory. If one wants to use a set of orbital elements to solve Kepler's problem , two additional parameters must be included. This is to say, in order to solve for the position and velocity of the orbiting object at an arbitrary future time, an extended set of eight orbital elements will be required.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p8\",\"quote\":\"When describing an orbit with orbital elements, typically two are needed to describe the size and shape of the trajectory, three are needed to describe the rotation of the orbit, and one is needed to describe the starting position along the orbit. These can then be extended to include an element describing the speed of motion, and an element describing the time that the starting position occurs if position as a function of time needs to be solved.\"}]}"
        }
